\f1\fs22 \'93Drugs?\'94 asked a Bubble Constable hopefully, as he returned his pistol under the waistband of his jeans and the tired elastic of his underwear. He\'92d left the safety catch off, ever presently at risk of becoming one sausage short of a barbie.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes,\'94 an Elvine answered. \'93Cane sugar and peanut butter.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I mean, who doesn\'92t love an Elvi song?\'94 was a common response as the Bubble Police clasped their shiny-bright tickets.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 They came down from their residential towers and up from everywhere else. Identifying Airborne versus Cloudborne versus Elvi was impossible, which was rather the point. The Airborne young dressed for the ground the way their trust fund patrons (who, in other cultures, were commonly referred to as 
\f2\i parents
\f1\i0 ) dressed for the opera\'97with research, and a stylist on retainer\'97end still managed to blend in. When they stepped onto actual grass, they did so with the tentative rapture of astronauts on a beach; many feel to their knees and stroked it. Some of the already munted picked greener-than-green blades and chewed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 The Cloudborne dressed in whatever glittered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi were a minority at their own festival, and many of them had volunteered to help marshal, bless, and direct foot traffic with karate hands. For everyone who could not be on the lawn, a live stream carried the day out to every Elvi in The Pale and the wider Ulyssian Meritocracy, and a great many of the uncloned besides.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Around the perimeter of the lawn, merch tents sold t-shirts that glittered like mirror balls, capes in all sizes, ponchos, pompadour wigs, and stick-on sideburns with adhesive that was already negotiating release by noon. One item on sale was priced for Airborne allowance accounts only: the exclusive, collectible Rock Against Revival Air Guitar. It came in its own festival-branded soft case\'97emblazoned with the tag, 
\f2\i thank you very much
\f1\i0 , allegedly in the hand of the High Elvis himself\'97which turned out to contain nothing more than stickers, a bag of plectrums, and enough pumped-in air to inflate the case to guitar proportions. When a button marked 
\f3\b\fs18 UNLOCK
\f1\b0\fs22   was pressed, the case released its air through six pipes (a deluxe edition had twelve) that collectively wheezed either a low, flatulent, magnificent drop D power chord or a B
\f4\fs18 \super \'81\'f3
\f1\fs22 \nosupersub  minor major 13
\fs14 \super th
\fs22 \nosupersub  \'93spy chord\'94 for jazz enthusiasts. The Airborne young flocked to the merch tables, recognising exclusivity when they saw it: a personal item comfortably priced higher, gram-for-gram, than Eastern Suburbs real estate, but with one significant improvement over bricks and mortar. It came with a strap.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Rock Against Revival Air Guitar sold out by lunchtime, even the jazz versions. The Airborne kids, far from feeling ripped off, laughed at the joke, strapped the bag over their shoulders, and went about playing air guitar at everyone that didn\'92t have one. One especially enterprising Airborne kid had an air guitar derivative instrument registered on the nation\'92s commodity exchange by close of business.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bar tents offered Pepsi and cocktails, with names such as the Blue Howahya, the Chunk o\'92 Churning Lava Butterscotch, and the Cadillac Malt. A milky thing called Love Me Condensed was served in a plastic boot. What the drinks had in common was a reliance on banana, peanut butter, condensed milk, a bacon-salt rim, and a design sensibility that made each look like a dessert in a moat. The Cloudborne kids, raised on whatever was going, handled them fine. The Airborne kids, raised at altitude on optimised macro-nutritious slushies invented by life-style influencers, had no antibodies for any of it, and went round-for-round with their Cloudborne contemporaries anyway.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The first band, The Tupelo Trio, opened with a tune that in a straighter universe would have answered to \'93That\'92s All Right\'94. The sound system, admitting the truth about itself for the first time, thumped the opening chord through half a million sternums at once. The surrounding fig trees gave up their entire holding of fruit bats in one black exhalation, and every picnic rug on the lawn stood up with its owners attached. The Domain had heard louder things in its time\'97such as the 1812 Overture back when the cannon were real, and that Funeral-Gabber DJ duo that we can\'92t name for legal reasons and because they threatened to play in our streets if we did.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Domain had never heard anything this loud and this happy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The trouble arrived with the third act, and the trouble was the lyrics.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Four lines into a song that all of you in more stately universes would recall and instantly tap your toes to, three Elvi came from stage-left at a dead sprint to argue using karate. The crowd roared with delight as the frontman toppled into the photographers\'92 pit and his sunglasses went into the fourth row. The crew rescued the microphones first and the singer second. The band resumed playing\'97for about sixteen bars until the fight broke out again. A roadie hauled the frontman clear by the collar but, by that time, the set was a wash. The stage manager shot a look at the abandoned instruments on stage that would have curdled a Love Me Condensed in its own boot, then consulted her clip board for the next-up act.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The squabbling became a pattern into the afternoon. Some acts surrendered their set time by punching on before they\'92d ascended to the stage. In the hospitality marquee behind the stage, bands with the Teacher\'92s words started eating on the left, while bands intending to freelance ate on the right. Even that didn\'92t stop fights breaking out, although combatants possessed enough civility to hang up their capes first, because there was justifiable violence and then there was dry-cleaning. Trays of fried sandwiches fell to the grass as grown Elvi, often in complementary jumpsuits, rolled under trestles, throwing short punches and quoting mondegreens as if they were scripture. Past the rider tables, at the hospitality marquee\'92s far end, a ring of Elvisettes, and a general atmosphere of exemption, marked where Chanta, the Great Teacher himself, was sitting the whole question out. Nobody threw a punch nor rolled anywhere near him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As the program progressed, the size of the bands increased, including keyboards then their first horn; by mid afternoon a band had backing singers and a someone whose entire job was tambourine. The day warmed to the temperature the drinks had been waiting for. Airborne teenagers, who had taken too many cocktails and too much sun, folded neatly at the tree line.  Felled by sweetness and saturated fat in an environment their bodies had never been consulted about, they gave back condensed milk and peanuts. The St Johns volunteers, who had prepared for crush injuries and misadventure, worked through the afternoon on a casualty profile their incident forms had no box for and settled for ticking 
\fs18 ALLERGIC REACTION
\fs22 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The punch-ups continued. Who could say who forgot what lyrics and who chose to chance their own? Every deviation emptied the side-stages; festival goers climbed up and fought with bouncers and musicians alike. The crowd loved the double act of concert and fight club, and booed the sound crew whenever the microphones came away too promptly. The brouhaha spilled into the wider audience after a 
\f2\i Tickle Me 
\f1\i0 series Elvine sang \'93is your arse filled with paint\'94, and almost every Elvis and Elvine clamoured to the front of the crowd and tried to mount the stage. Deep in the middle of the lawn, a few hundred Airborne kids mounted a punch-up of their own: slow-motion, pulled karate, a boy in a certified authentic poncho selling a knockout so operatic that strangers carried him overhead like a fallen king while his friends fanned him with Air Guitar cases. The fighting was, as far as they could tell, included in the ticket, which itself was a license to participate.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When a band including a full brass section opened, and their three lead singers decided to sing their own lyrics over the top of one another, the hospitality marquee emptied (apart from Chanta and the Elvisettes) and joined the on-stage riot. Even the production command centre cleared out. On stage, the horn section defended its riser like a hill fort, still playing while they kicked assailants down. Almost every Elvis in the crowd was in a punch-up and Airborne kids quickly started running books on the outcomes. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis walked on stage without fanfare, which on that day was itself a special effect. He came on at a stroll, in a suit that was doing none of the shouting, stepped around two colleagues wrestling on the stage deck, picked a fallen microphone off the boards and tapped it at four beats a second until he heard the sound of it echoing across The Domain like a gravel-throated metronome. He kept tapping, as patient as a claims assessor, while the punch-up around him became, fist by fist, self-conscious. The crowd fell quiet, apart form the Airborne kids throwing up at the tree line.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis recited the Oath of the Warranty, which solemnised the Elvi while baffling everyone else.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Everyone here loves the music,\'94 the High Elvis went on. \'93Why else would we be here? But we Elvi need to set a good example for our younger fans. What we need is a little more legislation; we need to lay a little law down. I hereby decree three new ones for Elvi to abide by, and for our friends and fans to observe as a mark of solidarity and respect.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>All the jumbotrons around the arena lit up; the High Elvis read them as they cycled through three slides.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Number 1: Sing the words of The Great Teacher.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Number 2: Do not punch up on anyone who does not sing the words of the Great Teacher.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Number 3: Sing the words of The Great Teacher.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Thank you very much.\'94
